// -------------------------------------------------------------
// Purpose of the Laboratory Work
// -------------------------------------------------------------
= Purpose of the Laboratory Work

1. Understand the structural design and low-level data structures of the xv6 file system, which models modern Unix filesystems (such as ext4) in a compact, readable codebase.
2. Decode xv6 directory listings and inspect file types, inode assignments, and byte sizes directly from `kernel/stat.h`.
3. Implement `user/mystat.c`, a custom user-space utility that invokes the `stat()` system call to inspect file metadata (type, inode number, link count, and byte size).
4. Trace hard link lifecycle mechanics inside the xv6 kernel, specifically inspecting `kernel/sysfile.c` (`sys_unlink`) and understanding when data blocks are deallocated in `kernel/fs.c` (`iput`/`itrunc`).
5. Analyze xv6 filesystem limits and disk geometry defined in `kernel/fs.h`, verifying directory name truncation (`DIRSIZ = 14`), computing maximum file capacity, and auditing the on-disk metadata layout generated by `mkfs`.
6. Implement `user/find.c`, a recursive directory traversal tool based on MIT 6.1810, properly handling entry streams, preventing recursion loops, and closing file descriptors to stay within descriptor boundaries.
7. Implement an optional `-n` stretch flag to inspect inode numbers, demonstrating that hard links share the exact inode of their target while nested regular files receive separate inodes.

