= Part 2. Write `mystat`

== 1. Implementation of `user/mystat.c`

The `stat()` library call in xv6 populates a user-space `struct stat` defined in `kernel/stat.h`:

```c
struct stat {
  int dev;     // File system's disk device
  uint ino;    // Inode number
  short type;  // Type of file
  short nlink; // Number of links to file
  uint64 size; // Size of file in bytes
};
```

We implemented `user/mystat.c` to iterate over path arguments, query metadata via `stat()`, decode the integer type into human-readable text, and output the required formatted string:

```c
#include "kernel/types.h"
#include "kernel/stat.h"
#include "user/user.h"

char *
typename(int t)
{
  if(t == T_DIR)
    return "directory";
  if(t == T_FILE)
    return "file";
  if(t == T_DEVICE)
    return "device";
  return "?";
}

int
main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
  struct stat st;
  if(argc < 2){
    fprintf(2, "usage: mystat <path>...\n");
    exit(1);
  }
  for(int i = 1; i < argc; i++){
    if(stat(argv[i], &st) < 0){
      fprintf(2, "mystat: cannot stat %s\n", argv[i]);
      continue;
    }
    printf("%s: %s, inode %d, links %d, size %d bytes\n",
           argv[i], typename(st.type), st.ino, st.nlink, (int)st.size);
  }
  exit(0);
}
```

== 2. Watching Hard Links at Work

Executing `mystat` across file operations:

```bash
$ mystat README . console
README: file, inode 2, links 1, size 2441 bytes
.: directory, inode 1, links 2, size 1024 bytes
console: device, inode 27, links 1, size 0 bytes

$ ln README r2
$ mystat README r2
README: file, inode 2, links 2, size 2441 bytes
r2: file, inode 2, links 2, size 2441 bytes

$ rm README
$ mystat r2
r2: file, inode 2, links 1, size 2441 bytes

$ cat r2 | wc
48 336 2441
```

== 3. Observe Answers for Part 2

#block(
  fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
  stroke: 0.5pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
  inset: 10pt,
  radius: 4pt,
  [
    *1. Outputs after `ln` and after `rm`. What changed and what did not?*
    - *What changed:* After `ln README r2`, the hard link counter (`links`) of inode 2 incremented from 1 to 2. Both `README` and `r2` refer to the identical inode 2. After `rm README`, the link counter decremented from 2 back to 1.
    - *What did not change:* The inode number (*2*), file type (*file*), byte size (*2441 bytes*), and contents (*48 lines, 336 words, 2441 bytes*) remained unchanged.

    *2. Find the line in `kernel/sysfile.c` (`sys_unlink`) that decrements the link count. When are the data blocks freed?*
    In `kernel/sysfile.c`, line *244*:
    ```c
    ip->nlink--;
    iupdate(ip);
    ```
    *When data blocks are freed:* Data blocks are not deallocated by `sys_unlink`. Data blocks are only returned to the free block pool inside `iput(ip)` in `kernel/fs.c` via `itrunc(ip)` when two conditions are met:
    1. `ip->nlink == 0` (no directory entries refer to the inode).
    2. `ip->ref == 1` (no open file descriptors hold an active reference to the inode).

    *3. xv6 has no symbolic links and no permissions. Look at `struct dinode` in `kernel/fs.h`: which fields does an xv6 inode have?*
    In `kernel/fs.h`:
    ```c
    struct dinode {
      short type;              // File type
      short major;             // Major device number (T_DEVICE only)
      short minor;             // Minor device number (T_DEVICE only)
      short nlink;             // Number of links to inode in file system
      uint size;               // Size of file (bytes)
      uint addrs[NDIRECT + 1]; // Data block addresses
    };
    ```
    - *Fields present:* `type`, `major`, `minor`, `nlink`, `size`, and `addrs[13]` (12 direct + 1 indirect pointer).
    - *Fields absent compared to Linux:* No user UID, no group GID, no mode/permission bits, no timestamps (`atime`, `mtime`, `ctime`), and no symlink target buffer.
  ]
)

